dsh: reasoning:
Now I understand the task. I am to act as 孫子 (Sun Tzu), applying my final list of 20 items, and answer the review questions. The output must be exactly the format specified, no preamble.

Let me carefully review the question. The design is a source-of-truth memory design for AI agents. The questions:

1. Will an agent follow it? Name anything that would make an agent misread, skip, or contradict a requirement.
2. The wording the COO wrote (listed under Facts) — keep, change, or drop each, and why.
3. Who reviews the judged checks? Most records have `check: reviewer`; the YAML says the project owner names who reviews, and nobody is named. Recommend what the design should say.
4. The two open `who_decides`: W3.6 (whether a procedure can be a script) and Rd3.1a (which agents work on the project, and so the strictest INDEX.md limit). Recommend who decides each, or say they should stay unassigned.
5. Three open behaviour questions: (a) may an entry be retired with nothing replacing it (K4.1 requires a superseded_by link)? (b) K1.1 says "change the entry first, then the code", while K4.3 says a claim is never rewritten — is the supersession step clear enough? (c) what work stops when a MUST fails — the whole task, or only the part the record governs?

I need to answer through my rules. Every step names the item it uses: "my #4". A step with no item is my own reading.

Let me think carefully as Sun Tzu applying the 20 items. The key rules that apply to a review of a design/memory system:

- #1 Reckon before battle: five matters, seven comparisons. Before changing the memory, lay out the design; compare. "多算勝，少算不勝" — more reckoning wins. Also the ordered chain 度→量→數→稱→勝: ground gives measurement... This applies: the design has no named limit; measurement decides. This is directly relevant to Rd3.1a (the INDEX.md size limit), which is a 量/數 matter. Compare the two sides on seven questions — for a design review, the "two sides" would be the reader/agent vs the design.
- #2 Make yourself unbeatable first; win then seek battle. Do not rely on enemy not attacking; rely on readiness. This applies to: don't rely on an agent not misreading — build the design so misreading is caught (tool checks). "勝可知而不可為" — victory can be known, not made. Relevant to "will an agent follow it": you cannot make an agent follow; you can only make failure detectable.
- #3 Whole over broken; strike plans first, cities last. Prefer whole over broken. This applies to the memory design: prefer the smallest change that keeps the design whole; the fix should be at the plan level (the requirements/prose) not patching symptoms. Also "上兵伐謀" — the highest is to strike plans: fix the source (the YAML/prose), not each agent's behavior.
- #4 Know whether you can fight; know the other and yourself. Know when a rule can and cannot be applied; the five ways to know victory. Relevant: the design needs to know whether a rule can be checked (reviewer vs tool), and "knowing whether your troops can attack but not whether the enemy can be attacked" = half a victory. The "enemy" here is agent misreading. Half-victory = a record with only a reviewer check.
- #5 Move only on advantage; never from anger. Do not add a rule without advantage/gain. Irreversibility. Relevant: don't add structure without advantage; don't fill gaps with invented values.
- #6 Mix harm into benefit. Deliberate with both. Relevant: every recommendation names its risk/price (the brief requires this anyway). Also "不盡知用兵之害者，則不能盡知用兵之利" — whoever doesn't know the harm can't know the benefit. The design must say what breaks if a MUST fails — question 5(c) is precisely harm.
- #7 Deception. Hmm, less relevant. Could be used: the design must not let an agent's appearance of compliance substitute for compliance. "能而示之不能" — not really. Maybe for the reviewer check: a reviewer is a legible check that can be gamed; a tool check is harder to fake. My reading.
- #8 Value victory not duration; speed. Relevant: keep the design short and fast; the INDEX.md size limit is a speed/endurance matter. Also "取用於國，因糧於敵" — take provisions from the enemy: the design should reuse what already exists (the existing docs/ tools) rather than duplicating. Also W3.6: procedures live as scripts.
- #9 Bring the other side to you; do not be brought. Relevant: the design should bring the agent to the reading, not rely on the agent to come — i.e., load at session start via hooks/imports (Rd2.1). Also: make the required path the easy path.
- #10 Avoid the solid, strike the empty; no constant form. Relevant: don't fight where the design is strong (the things the tool already checks); strike the empty (where no check exists — e.g., the reviewer checks, the unnamed reviewer, the size limit). Also no constant form: the design must adapt to each project (which agents work on it), so the limit is a comparison.
- #11 Foreknowledge must come from people; pay for it. Relevant: the reviewer is the human/people source; the design must name and pay for the reviewer. Also: the build script's checks don't show content is right — foreknowledge must come from people, not from the tool alone. This is directly relevant to question 3: the reviewer checks need named people/agents; a tool is the 度/數, not foreknowledge. "不可取於鬼神，不可象於事，不可驗於度，必取於人" — cannot be verified by measurement; must be taken from people. Strong basis for question 3: reviewer checks must have named reviewers; and for Rd3.1a, the limit must be measured but the choice of agents is a person's.
- #12 Seek it in 勢, made of regular and irregular, not from the people. Relevant: don't demand good behavior of agents (責於人); change the structure (勢). This is the core of question 1: an agent misreading is not fixed by exhortation but by changing the layout/structure (regular and irregular: tool checks as 正, reviewer as 奇). "求之於勢，不責於人" — seek it in the design, not blame the agent. Strong basis for recommendations: make the requirements machine-checkable rather than telling the agent to be careful.
- #13 Not every road is taken, and not every command of the ruler is accepted. Relevant: the general may refuse a command that means no victory. This applies to the agent: a MUST can be refused only where the way means certain failure... Hmm, more directly: the design should not accept every command; some roads not taken. For question 5(c): "what work stops when a MUST fails" — the general's discretion: don't stop a whole task for a local failure if the way of battle means sure victory. But also "戰道必勝，主曰無戰，必戰可也" — fighting is permissible when sure victory. This is a genuinely tricky one to apply without inventing. My reading of #13 for 5(c): a MUST failure stops the work it governs, not the whole task — because the general protects the whole mission and does not accept a command that destroys it. But actually #13 is about the ruler's commands, not about a gate. Let me be careful. The brief demands: "Do not invent facts." I can cite #13 for the principle that not every command is absolute — the record's own scope governs.
- #14 Choose the action by the ratio of forces. Relevant: scale the check to the number of agents working; the strictest limit ratio. "十則圍之" — with overwhelming force, surround. For Rd3.1a: the design should choose by the ratio — the limit is set by the strictest agent among those working. Also "兵非貴益多也" — numbers not valued for their own sake. Relevant to 64 records: don't value more requirements for their own sake; "惟無武進，足以併力、料敵、取人而已" — enough to concentrate strength, size up the enemy and take men. So 64 records from 46 requirements: is that more? My reading says the split adds clauses but must not multiply gates — compare the count.
- #15 The general's faults: five dangers, six defeats. Relevant: the design's own decision style has faults: "愛民，可煩也" — loving the people can be harried; a design that tries to be too kind/careful can be troubled (e.g., too many SHOULD exceptions weaken gates). "必死可殺" — a design set on dying (a rigid MUST) can be killed. The general's faults are levers: the design's five dangers. Also 六敗: the six kinds of defeat — "將不能料敵，以少合衆，以弱擊強，兵無選鋒，曰北" — a general who cannot size up the enemy, meets many with few and has no picked vanguard, is rout. Applied: a design that puts the strongest agents (reviewer) against all 64 records with no picked vanguard... Hmm. More directly: the five dangers = the design has virtues that are levers against it. E.g. "loving the people" (wanting every rule to be clear and kind) can be used to harry it into too many SHOULDs. "廉潔可辱" — scrupulous about honor can be shamed: a design that insists on correctness can be shamed into adding rules.
- #16 Orders clear first, then enforced; care that cannot command is useless. DIRECTLY relevant. "約束不明，申令不熟，將之罪也" — if orders are not clear and not drilled in, it's the general's fault. Applied to question 1 and question 3: if the requirements are unclear (misread), that's the design's fault, not the agent's. "卒未親附而罰之則不服" — punish before attachment fails. Applied: don't gate (MUST) before the agent is attached to the design (has the reading loaded), i.e., session-start loading must come before enforcement. "厚而不能使，愛而不能令...譬若驕子" — care without command is useless: a design full of guidance/SHOULD without enforcement is the spoiled child.
- #17 Learn the enemy by probing and by signs. DIRECTLY relevant to question 1: you learn whether an agent follows by probing it and reading signs, not by assuming. "策之而知得失之計，作之而知動靜之理，形之而知死生之地，角之而知有餘不足之處." Applied: the design has no probe — EJ skipped the test of whether agents follow the YAML better than Markdown (not measured). The build script only checks shape. So the answer to "will an agent follow it?" is: unknown, because no probe. Recommend a probe. Also read the signs: the review's own findings are signs.
- #18 Spirit, heart, strength and change. Relevant: manage the design's spirit (session-start read), heart (reviewer), strength (tool checks), change (adaptation to each project). Eight prescriptions: don't push a desperate enemy / leave a gap for a surrounded army. Applied: leave a gap — don't close every loophole; a design that traps agents with no written reason escape produces fabricated reasons. "圍師必闕" — leave a gap for a surrounded army. This is a strong and useful rule for question 5(a) and 5(c): allow a path out (retire with nothing replacing it; stop only the governed work), because a design with no exit produces worse behavior (agents route around it or falsify). Also "餌兵勿食" — don't take bait: don't take the bait of adding a rule for every edge case.
- #19 Name the ground, act as prescribed; troops with no way out fight. Relevant: identify the situation (which requirement level, which check) then act as prescribed. "投之亡地然後存" — with no way out they fight. Applied to 5(c): if a MUST failure stops the whole task, the agent is in dead ground and fights (or breaks); if it stops only the governed work, there is a way out. Also "九地之變...不可不察也" — the variations must be examined: the design must vary by project.
- #20 Which house falls first: judge by the land unit, the tax, and what they breed. Relevant: judge a project's memory design by its structure (land unit, tax = the limits and the burden) and what it breeds (arrogant ruler, wasteful ministers = too many rules; empty coffers and few officials = few but effective rules). Zhao held because it kept its old laws, frugal ruler, humble ministers over a prosperous people. Applied: the design should keep its old laws (existing docs, existing tools) and be frugal in new structure. This is a nice basis for "drop what you can". "趙毋失其故法，晉國歸焉" — Zhao did not lose its old laws, and Jin went to it.

OK. Now, the actual substance. Let me now derive concrete findings for each question. This is a review task; I need real, checkable observations about the two files. Let me carefully examine the YAML and prose for inconsistencies. Let me re-read specific parts.

Let me collect concrete issues.

**Question 1: Will an agent follow it? What would make an agent misread, skip, or contradict a requirement.**

Potential issues in the YAML layout:

a) `how_to_read` says "Read level first, then text. When a clause and its requirement seem to differ, text governs." But **there is no requirement text at the parent level** — the parent ids (W1.1, W1.2, W3.3, W4.1, W5.3, W6.1, W6.3, K6.1, K6.3, Rd2.3, Rd3.1, Rd4.5) exist only as `part_of` references. The YAML has no record for the parent requirement. So "text governs" has nothing to govern between — a clause has no parent text to differ from. An agent looking for the requirement's text finds only clause records. This is a real misread risk. Let me verify: W1 has requirements W1.1a, W1.1b, W1.1c — no W1.1 record. Indeed there's no `id: W1.1`. So `part_of: W1.1` points to a nonexistent record. Similarly `links` resolve? W1.1a links [W2, W3.6, K4.1] — W2 is a group id (exists as a group title), W3.6 exists, K4.1 exists. But the build script says "every link resolves" — how? W2 is a group. Hmm, maybe links resolve against groups too. But `part_of: W1.1` — W1.1 is neither a record nor a group id. Let me check the groups: W1, W2, W3, W4, W5, W6, K1, K4, K5, K6, Rd1, Rd2, Rd3, Rd4, Rd5, Rd6. So W1.1 is not a group. So `part_of` points to a nonexistent target. That's a concrete finding: an agent reading `part_of: W1.1` cannot find W1.1's text; the "requirement" it belongs to was never written down as text. This directly answers Q1 and Q2 (the `how_to_read` line).

Wait — the brief says "requirements holding clauses at different levels were split into clause records (W1.1a, W1.1b…, `part_of` names the requirement)". And "all 46 requirements are covered". Covered may mean each of 46 requirement ids appears as a `part_of` or as a record id. So the parent text may have been discarded. The prose carries the requirement's meaning maybe. But `how_to_read` says "Read level first, then text. When a clause and its requirement seem to differ, text governs." If the requirement has no text in the YAML, then "text" can only mean the clause's own text ("text" is the field name). Actually re-read: "Read level first, then text. When a clause and its requirement seem to differ, text governs." Hmm, ambiguous. "text" could mean the record's `text` field. Then "when a clause and its requirement seem to differ" — but the requirement isn't in the file. So this is a genuine ambiguity. My reading: the line is unimplementable as written; either name the parent's text or drop the line.

Also the prose says "46 requirements, grouped by what happens to a memory". The YAML `how_to_read` says "Each record is one requirement, or one clause of one". But 64 records from 46 requirements. An agent told "each record is one requirement" will count 64 requirements, not 46. Misread. Also `how_to_read` contains "There are no fixed numbers: where a limit applies, it is a comparison that measurement decides." — an agent may read this as "no numbers anywhere" and skip the `done:` time requirement or the id's six letters. Weak words.

b) The `levels` definition: "MUST: A gate. When it fails, the work it governs stops." But what work stops is exactly question 5(c) — the YAML doesn't say whether "the work it governs" is the whole task or the part. An agent reading "the work it governs stops" might stop the whole task. This is a contradiction/ambiguity between the levels header and the records (some records' `fails_if` is local). Also the prose's "Must" isn't defined there; the strategy table says MUST definition.

c) `checks: reviewer` says "a person or agent reading the work judges it. The project owner names who reviews." But no record names a reviewer, and `who_decides: none` on all reviewer records. So for every reviewer check, there's no name. An agent asked "who checks this?" finds `check: reviewer`, `who_decides: none`, and the header says the project owner names who reviews — but the project owner hasn't. So the check is unowned. Concrete.

Also: `check: reviewer` but `who_decides: none` for many. The header says the owner names who reviews, but the record's `who_decides` is `none`, which per `how_to_read` means "the record leaves no choice open". So the record contradicts the header: the header says there's an open choice (who reviews), the record says none. An agent will follow the record-level field (more specific) and conclude no choice is open. This is a real between-prose-and-YAML contradiction. Good finding.

d) `who_decides` values are free text, not a controlled vocabulary: `none`, `project owner`, `unassigned: whether a procedure can be a script`, `the agent doing the read: what is worth keeping`, `the project's workflow: when work goes the other way`. `how_to_read` says "none means ...; unassigned means ...". But the other values embed the question after a colon. An agent checking `who_decides == 'unassigned'` (as a tool might) will fail on `unassigned: whether...`. There's no `who_decides` key list; W3.6 and Rd3.1a use `'unassigned: ...'` while the header defines bare `unassigned`. Inconsistent. Concrete. Also `the project's workflow` and `the project owner` — the roles section names `project owner` but not `the project's workflow`. So a reader can't resolve half the values against `roles`.

e) `retired_ids: >-` is a scalar with a sentence "K2 and K3 are not used. Other text cites K4 to K6, so the numbers stay." But K2 and K3 don't appear anywhere as records or groups; there's no K2, K3 group. So `retired_ids` is a prose note, not a list. An agent might try to parse it as a list of ids. Minor layout issue. Actually more importantly: the group numbering jumps W1→W6, K1→K6 missing K2, K3, Rd1→Rd6. The header explains K2/K3 retired but not the missing groups... K2, K3 retired. Fine. But the prose cites K4.3 etc. Fine.

f) `basis` values: `basis: 'd: What will be put in'` and `basis: 'v; d: What it is for'`. The `basis` header says `v:` = vision file, `d:` = this design's text (the section named). But the group `W2` basis is `'v; d: What it is for'` — `v` is bare, not `v: <section>`. And records have no `basis` at all — only groups do. So a record's basis is inherited from the group, not stated. An agent reading a record can't find its basis. Concrete: record-level `basis` is absent; the header doesn't say basis is inherited from the group. The build script's "every record has the same keys" — let me check: does every record have `basis`? Looking at W1.1a: keys are id, part_of, level, text, fails_if, check, who_decides, links. No basis. Groups have basis. So "every record has the same keys" is true (records don't have basis), but "all 46 requirements covered" is about ids. Fine. But the header's `basis` key is unexplained as group-only.

g) The `links` are inconsistent in direction: W1.1a links [W2, W3.6, K4.1]; W6.3b links [K6.3, K6.3b] — K6.3 is a group and K6.3b is the record itself? No, K6.3b's links include K6.3b (itself). That's a self-link. Let me check: K6.3b is `part_of: K6.3`, `links: [K6.3, K6.3b]`. Yes — K6.3b links to itself. That's a concrete defect: a self-link. Also W6.3b `fails_if: as K6.3b` and links [K6.3, K6.3b] — wait, W6.3b says `fails_if: as K6.3b`, but K6.3b is the "INDEX.md is never merged by hand" SHOULD. W6.3b's text is "A conflict in INDEX.md is resolved by regenerating it (K6.3)." Its fails_if "as K6.3b" — but K6.3b is at K6; yes W6.3b refers to K6.3b, which exists. Hmm, but its links list [K6.3, K6.3b] — the link to itself in W6.3b? No, W6.3b links to K6.3b (a different record). Let me recheck K6.3b: links: [K6.3, K6.3b] — that's K6.3 group and itself K6.3b. Yes, self-link in K6.3b. Concrete.

Also W6.3b's `fails_if: as K6.3b` — it says its failure is "as K6.3b", but K6.3b's fails_if is "INDEX.md is merged by hand, with no written reason" while W6.3b's text says "resolve by regenerating it". Actually W6.3b is a duplicate of K6.3b: W6.3b text "A conflict in INDEX.md is resolved by regenerating it (K6.3)" ↔ K6.3b text "INDEX.md is never merged by hand: after the entry files, regenerate it and commit it with them." These are duplicates across two groups (W6 and K6). Duplication risk: an agent may change one and not the other. Concrete finding: W6.3b and K6.3b are the same requirement twice; W6.3b defers to K6.3b for its failure. Duplicate requirements violate "one thing" and can drift. Good.

Also W6.3a text: "whoever merges keeps both sides... Two entries that each supersede the same entry are reported..." check git; reviewer. Fine.

h) `checks` header lists `tool`, `git`, `reviewer`, `secret scanner`, `none`. But records use `check: tool; git`, `check: reviewer; git`, `check: secret scanner; reviewer`. The header defines each label singularly, not the `;` composition. Minor: an agent parsing `check` needs to split on `; `. The header doesn't say that. Also `none` says "guidance: nothing to check" — but some `guidance` records have `check: none`, good. But some non-guidance records also use `none`? Let me check: W2.1a guidance check none. W2.2a guidance none. W4.1a guidance none. K4.2 guidance none. Rd3.1b guidance none. Rd4.2/4.3/4.5a guidance none. Rd5.4, Rd5.5 guidance none. Good. But `fails_if: nothing: a definition.` for guidance. Fine.

i) The prose says "The requirements are in SOURCE_OF_TRUTH_REQUIREMENTS.yaml, beside this file: 46 requirements". But the YAML has 64 records and no count. An agent counting records gets 64. If it reports "46 requirements" it's wrong about records; if it treats each record as a requirement, it's 64. Also the prose cites ids like "W1.2, K6.3, Rd5.3" — W1.2 is not a record (only W1.2a, W1.2b). K6.3 is a group, Rd5.3 is a record. So the prose's own citations mix group ids and record ids. An agent searching the YAML for "W1.2" finds no record — only W1.2a/b. That's a concrete misread: the prose cites requirement ids that aren't in the YAML. Let me verify the prose citations: "Its header... (W1.2)" ✓ no record W1.2. "(W5.3)" no record W5.3 (only W5.3a/b). "K4.3" is a record ✓. "W3.6" ✓ record. "Rd4.5" no record (only Rd4.5a/b). "W1.2" no record. "K6.2" ✓. "K6.3" group. "W6.3" group. "Rd5.4" ✓. "Rd2.1" record. "Rd2.2" record. "Rd2.3" group. "Rd4" group. "Rd3" group. "K6" group. "W3.1" ✓. "W5.3" group. "K1.1" ✓. "Rd5.3" ✓. "Rd4.5" group. "Rd5" group. So several prose citations resolve only to groups or to nothing. This is a real cross-file inconsistency. Good finding for Q1.

j) The prose says the entry header holds keys including `covers`, `source`, `depends_on`, `supersedes`, `superseded_by`, `changed:`, `done:`. The YAML W1.2b lists the same keys. But the prose also says "`id`, `kind`, `status`, `author`, the date it was recorded (`recorded`)". Consistent. But the prose says "on a decision to build something once its work is complete, `done:`" — W1.2b says "A decision to build something, once its work is complete, also holds `done:`". Consistent.

k) The tool checks: "the id form; prefix against kind; kind one of five; status one of three; the `---` header lines; `**Why:**` line and a claim in active entries; conflict-marker lines; duplicate ids; INDEX.md equal to what it builds, and its size limit." Note: W1.1a says "It matches the entry's `kind`" and "An entry's kind, and so its prefix, never changes" — the tool cannot check "never changes" except via git rename. W1.1a `check: tool; git` and fails_if includes "git shows an entry file renamed to a different prefix". OK.

l) W1.1b says id is `DD-MM-xxxxxx-YYYY` with six random lowercase letters and digits, example `13-10-k3f7a2-2026`. But the id form in the fact sheet is "the id form" checked by tool. The pattern `DD-MM-xxxxxx-YYYY` is day-month-year but the example `13-10-k3f7a2-2026` = day 13, month 10, year 2026. Fine. But note the prose says "Its header... holds: `id`". W1.1b text says "the file keeps its name" and W1.1c says filename is `project-memory/entries/<prefix>-<id>-<slug>.md`. So the id embedded in the filename includes DD-MM-xxxxxx-YYYY, and the file also has `id`. Fine.

m) `status` values: `active`, `superseded`, `retired`. W4.1b MUST. K4.1 MUST says superseded or retired entries "links to what replaced it (`superseded_by`)". But a `retired` entry by definition may have nothing replacing it. K4.1's text bundles superseded and retired: "It is marked `superseded` or `retired`, links to what replaced it (`superseded_by`)". And fails_if: "a `superseded` or `retired` entry with no link." This is question 5(a). The prose says "An entry is never deleted: it is marked `superseded` or `retired` and linked to what replaced it." Same bundle. An agent retiring an entry with no replacement cannot satisfy K4.1 and must either invent a link or use `superseded` wrongly. Concrete.

n) K4.3 says "After an entry is first committed, it only gains a `changed:` line, a new `status`, the `superseded_by` link, and `done:` on a decision to build something". But W1.2a requires `author`, `recorded`, etc.; K4.3 doesn't mention `supersedes` on the new entry (the new entry has `supersedes`), nor `covers` updates. Also, the prose says a changed claim is a new entry that supersedes the old one, and the old only gains a `changed:` line, its new `status` and the `superseded_by` link. What about `done:`? K4.3 includes done. And what if an agent needs to correct a typo or add `covers`? K4.3 says it "only gains" those. Typo fixes would violate K4.3. Concrete contradiction between W4.1a ("Anyone may change any entry") and K4.3 ("only gains a changed line, new status, superseded_by, done"). A reader sees "anyone may change any entry" then K4.3 restricts it. Which governs? This is question 5(b). Good.

o) K1.1 says "To change behaviour that a decision or fact governs, change the entry first, then the code." But K4.3 says a claim is never rewritten: a changed claim is a new entry that supersedes the old. So "change the entry first" for a decision whose behaviour changes cannot mean edit the old claim; it means write a new entry that supersedes, then change the code. But K1.1's `fails_if` says "git shows a commit changing the behaviour a decision or fact governs, with no change to that entry in the same commit or an earlier one". "no change to that entry" — but under K4.3 the old entry is not the thing changed except status/link; the new entry is a different file. So the fails_if's phrase "that entry" is literally impossible to satisfy if the entry is superseded (the old entry only gains status/link; the change is a new entry). An agent will read "change to that entry" and edit the old entry's claim — exactly what K4.3 forbids. Concrete contradiction. Good.

Also: K1.1 says "Work that has to go the other way is a matter for the project's workflow, not for memory." And `who_decides: the project's workflow: when work goes the other way`. But the roles section doesn't define "the project's workflow". Who is that? Unnamed. Concrete.

p) Rd5.3 says the reader writes a report in its task's `state.md` and tells the project owner. But `covers` and the mismatch — the report needs three items. Fine. Rd5.4 says two cases are not mismatches. Rd5.5 says Rd5.3 binds readers, not the owner. Consistent.

q) Rd4.1 MUST: "Before editing a file, an agent reads the entries whose `covers` include it." `check: reviewer`. But `covers` is a SHOULD-layout field (W1.2b is SHOULD). So an entry may legitimately lack `covers` (with a written reason), and then Rd4.1 cannot be satisfied. Not a contradiction per se but a gap: the MUST depends on a SHOULD field. Concrete.

r) Rd2.1 SHOULD: reads four things at session start. `fails_if`: "a start hook or instruction loads any memory file outside these four". But the prose also says at session start agents read `AGENTS.md`, `INDEX.md`, the `work/` listing and the task's `state.md`. `work/` listing is a directory listing, not a memory file. Fine. But note `AGENTS.md` is "standing instructions", not memory; the four "memory files" include AGENTS.md. Minor.

