**Question 5: three open behaviour questions.**

(a) may an entry be retired with nothing replacing it (K4.1 requires a superseded_by link)?
   - Text: K4.1 bundles superseded and retired with a link. The prose: "An entry is never deleted: it is marked `superseded` or `retired` and linked to what replaced it."
   - My answer: Yes, retire with nothing replacing it must be allowed, otherwise `retired` is unusable — but K4.1 as written forbids it. Two options: (i) change K4.1 to require the link only for `superseded`, and require a written reason for `retired`; or (ii) drop the `retired` status and let everything be `superseded`. My rules: #5 move only on advantage — if `retired` has no distinct use, drop it (#15 "餌兵勿食" don't take the bait of two statuses); if it has a use (an entry that is obsolete with no replacement), then the link cannot be required. My #1: compare the two statuses on the seven questions — do they differ in who decides, what the reader does, what the check is? Under the design, both are "never loaded except by link", both are non-active, both need a link. So they are indistinguishable except the link. That's a "one thing" failure: two names for one state. Recommendation: either keep both and make `retired` link-free (so the distinction is the link), or drop `retired` and keep `superseded`. My recommendation: keep `retired`, change K4.1 so the link is required for `superseded` only; `retired` requires a written reason and no link. Reason: the reader behavior is the same, but the *writer's* claim differs (something replaced it vs nothing did), and the check can tell them apart. Cost: a `retired` entry with no link gives the reader no successor, so the reader must judge the old body as dead. Also update the prose line the same way. This is concrete and avoids inventing.
   - Also note W4.1b status list includes retired, and W1.1a/K4.1 etc.

(b) K1.1 "change the entry first, then the code" vs K4.3 "a claim is never rewritten" — is the supersession step clear enough?
   - My answer: No. K1.1's `fails_if` says "with no change to that entry" — but under K4.3 the old entry's claim cannot change and the new entry is a different file. An agent will either edit the old claim (violating K4.3) or change the code with no entry change and read that as a failure. The supersession step must be named where K1.1 is: "write a new entry that supersedes the old, then change the code" — and K1.1's `fails_if` should say "no new superseding entry (or first-time entry) in the same commit or an earlier one". My #16: orders clear first. #4: know whether you can fight — K1.1's check (`git; reviewer`) can't tell a first-time decision from a supersession as written. Recommendation: change K1.1's text and fails_if to name the supersession path explicitly and cross-link K4.3 and W1.1a; keep K1.1 as SHOULD (it already is). Cost: the wording gets longer; but the current wording is the one that produces the K4.3 violation.
   - Also point out: K4.3 says the old entry "only gains a `changed:` line, a new `status`, the `superseded_by` link, and `done:`". It should also allow the new `supersedes` link on the *new* entry (which is a new file, so fine) and should allow typo/format fixes? That's separate. But for (b), the key is to name the two-file mechanic.

(c) what work stops when a MUST fails — the whole task, or only the part the record governs?
   - Text: `levels: MUST: A gate. When it fails, the work it governs stops.` "the work it governs" is the ambiguous phrase.
   - My answer: only the part the record governs, not the whole task. My rules: #13 the general does not accept a command that means sure ruin; #19 "troops with no way out fight" — if a local MUST failure stops the whole task, the agent is in dead ground and will either abandon the task or route around the rule; #18 "圍師必闕" leave a gap. #2 "make yourself unbeatable... wait for the enemy"; #12 勢 not 責於人 — make the stop structural per record. But my #16 "orders clear": if the design intends the whole task to stop, it must say so. My recommendation: define it in `levels`: "MUST: a gate. When it fails, the work the record governs stops; the agent stops that work, records the failure, and carries on with work the record does not govern." Also the "work it governs" should be tied to the record's own scope: `covers`/the text. And a failing MUST that the owner must settle should not silently stop unrelated work. Cost: partial failures can hide behind local stops; the mitigation is the report (Rd5.3-style) and the tool check. Also note that the tool checks (id, header, INDEX) are repo-wide — a failing tool MUST does stop the commit/merge, which is the "work it governs" (the commit). Good nuance.

